## Executive Conclusion

The Samsung Galaxy Y (GT-S5360 / Totoro) is a severely constrained late-2011 ARMv6 device featuring a Broadcom BCM21553 SoC, ~290 MB of usable RAM, a 320×240 3.0" resistive-like capacitive display, and a 2.6.35.7 Android kernel.

Attempting an independent Linux boot, kernel recompile, or modern distribution port (Debian, Alpine, PostmarketOS) on this hardware is an **engineering trap**. Modern Linux userspaces (glibc 2.28+, systemd, modern TLS libraries) drop ARMv6 or require kernel interfaces (like `memfd_create`, `epoll_create1`, modern `futex`) that do not exist in Linux 2.6.35. Furthermore, BML/STL partition schemes and closed-source Broadcom VideoCore IV / Videocore graphics/baseband blobs make standalone OS maintenance near impossible without bricking the NAND.

**The Shortest, Safest, and Most Rewarding Path:**
Keep Android 2.3.6 running as the low-level hardware abstraction layer (HAL) and display driver. Run a **lightweight, custom static-musl ARMv6 userspace via an Android-assisted root chroot (or proot)** stored entirely on a loop device inside the 14 GB VFAT SD card.

By taking this hybrid approach, you achieve a fully functional Linux node in hours without touching flash partitions, risking bricks, or losing Wi-Fi and power management.

